## What to do
1. `git log --since=2026-09-02 -p -- <file>` per file to see exactly what the campaign changed. Look
   for: duplicated errno/terminal classification logic that should live in one place (POSDDD: one
   owner per rule — e.g. the ENOENT/NOT_FOUND transient-route rule in cpp, the WRITABLE/resubmit
   handling in node adapter), leftover diagnostics/dead branches/unused includes or imports introduced
   during debugging, avoidable allocations or copies on the send/poll hot path introduced by the
   fixes, magic numbers that should be named constants shared with existing ones, test helpers
   duplicated across the m6a/m6b/channel tests.
2. Apply only changes that keep behaviour identical and are locally verifiable. No public API or
   contract changes (`node-public-contract.json` must stay byte-identical; cpp exported headers
   unchanged except pure cleanup). Keep each language's diff small and reviewable.
3. Verify: cpp — build `framework/languages/cpp/build/linux-ninja-c-e2e` (`cmake --build ... -j2`) and
   run the four touched unit test binaries + `ctest -R 'channel_messaging|m6a|m6b|execution'`; node —
   `npm run build` and `npm test -- --testPathPattern 'contract/(backend-contract|stream-runtime|stream-session-runtime|sample-regression|node-binding-parity)'`
   (or the repo's equivalent) plus `npm run lint` on the touched packages. Env: `TMPDIR=/dev/shm/zlink-tmp-<lang>`
   (mkdir -p), `flock -w7200 /tmp/zlink-node-gate.lock` for node runs, `ZLINK_LIBRARY_PATH` unset for
   node (local packages installed). Do not rebuild Core or local packages.
4. If a candidate refactor would change behaviour or you are unsure, list it under "deferred" with
   file:line and reasoning instead of applying it.

